                <!-- Quantum Dot Technology Deep Dive -->
                <ProjectSection Title="Quantum Dot Technology" Icon="fas fa-atom">
                    <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">Quantum dots are semiconductor nanocrystals 2-10 nanometers across, containing 10-50 atoms. Their quantum mechanical properties are what make them useful for photonic quantum computing:</p>

                    <!-- Core Science -->
                    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-8">
                        <div class="bg-white/[.02] rounded-lg p-6 border border-cyan-600/25">
                            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-100 mb-4 flex items-center">
                                <i class="fas fa-microscope mr-3"></i>Fundamental Physics
                            </h3>
                            <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 text-sm">
                                <li><strong>Band gap:</strong> electrons are excited from the valence band to the conduction band</li>
                                <li><strong>Photon emission:</strong> when they fall back, they emit photons of a specific energy and wavelength</li>
                                <li><strong>Quantum confinement:</strong> the optical properties depend on the dot's size</li>
                                <li><strong>Photoluminescent efficiency:</strong> maximized by choosing the right coating materials</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <div class="bg-white/[.02] rounded-lg p-6 border border-line">
                            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-100 mb-4 flex items-center">
                                <i class="fas fa-industry mr-3"></i>Manufacturing Process
                            </h3>
                            <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 text-sm">
                                <li><strong>Molecular Beam Epitaxy (MBE):</strong> precise crystal growth under ultra-high vacuum</li>
                                <li><strong>Gallium arsenide substrate:</strong> a III-V semiconductor chosen for its performance</li>
                                <li><strong>Self-assembly:</strong> strain in the crystal makes the dots form as islands</li>
                                <li><strong>Capping layer:</strong> a protective coating that stops electrons escaping</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Key Advantages -->
                    <div class="bg-white/[.02] rounded-lg p-6 border border-line">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-100 mb-4">Critical Properties for Quantum Computing</h3>
                        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
                            <div>
                                <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-2">Identical Photon Generation</h4>
                                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Photonic quantum circuits rely on identical photons for quantum interference and entanglement.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div>
                                <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-2">Precise Timing Control</h4>
                                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Emitting photons in sync makes deterministic quantum gate operations possible.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div>
                                <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-2">Scalable Architecture</h4>
                                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">The dots can be built into large photonic quantum computing systems.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </ProjectSection>

                <!-- Photonic Quantum Computing Innovation -->
                <ProjectSection Title="Application: Photonic Quantum Computing" Icon="fas fa-lightbulb">
                    <div class="bg-white/[.02] rounded-lg p-6 border border-line mb-6">
                        <div class="flex items-center mb-4">
                            <div class="bg-cy/10 rounded-full w-3 h-3 mr-3"></div>
                            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-100">The Idea</h3>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                            Large technology companies were investing heavily in electron-based quantum computing. My report argued for a less explored route, <strong>photonic quantum computing</strong>, which computes with light instead of electrons. Quantum dots are the key component, because they generate the identical photons the approach depends on.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                        <div class="bg-white/[.02] rounded-lg p-6 border border-line">
                            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-100 mb-4">Hong-Ou-Mandel Effect</h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm mb-3">The quantum effect that photonic computation is built on:</p>
                            <ul class="text-gray-400 text-sm space-y-1">
                                <li>• Identical photons from quantum dots enter a beam splitter</li>
                                <li>• Quantum interference produces entangled photon pairs</li>
                                <li>• Their superposition states are used for computation</li>
                                <li>• Processing works through measurement</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="bg-white/[.02] rounded-lg p-6 border border-line">
                            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-100 mb-4">Competitive Advantages</h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm mb-3">Where the report argued it beats electron-based systems:</p>
                            <ul class="text-gray-400 text-sm space-y-1">
                                <li>• <strong>Speed of light:</strong> computation is carried by photons</li>
                                <li>• <strong>Room temperature:</strong> no cryogenic cooling needed</li>
                                <li>• <strong>Lower energy use:</strong> cheaper to run</li>
                                <li>• <strong>Scaling:</strong> a modular design that is easier to grow</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </ProjectSection>

